1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,340
A stone whirls on a string.

2
00:00:02,340 --> 00:00:04,173
The Moon circles the Earth.

3
00:00:04,173 --> 00:00:05,620
A car takes a bend.

4
00:00:06,020 --> 00:00:07,317
All three go round.

5
00:00:07,317 --> 00:00:09,453
So what keeps pulling each one in?

6
00:00:09,903 --> 00:00:13,396
Aarav takes the turn at the
college gate on his bicycle.

7
00:00:13,396 --> 00:00:14,846
Slowly, it is easy.

8
00:00:15,196 --> 00:00:17,281
Faster, and his tyres slide out.

9
00:00:17,281 --> 00:00:20,593
What holds him on the curve,
and why does it let go?

10
00:00:21,093 --> 00:00:25,503
By the end of this video, you will find
his top speed with one short formula.

11
00:00:26,153 --> 00:00:28,200
So what is a centripetal force?

12
00:00:28,550 --> 00:00:33,368
In episode forty eight, going round
needed an acceleration toward the centre,

13
00:00:33,368 --> 00:00:34,708
v squared over r.

14
00:00:35,108 --> 00:00:39,553
By Newton's second law, an acceleration
needs a net force the same way.

15
00:00:39,953 --> 00:00:43,313
That net force toward the
centre is the centripetal force.

16
00:00:43,913 --> 00:00:46,140
It is not a new kind of force.

17
00:00:46,140 --> 00:00:49,035
It is a job that some real force must do.

18
00:00:49,435 --> 00:00:50,703
A string's tension.

19
00:00:50,703 --> 00:00:52,019
The road's friction.

20
00:00:52,019 --> 00:00:52,919
Gravity.

21
00:00:52,696 --> 00:00:54,326
Or the push of a wall.

22
00:00:54,976 --> 00:00:59,122
A half kilogram stone on a one metre
string goes round a smooth table,

23
00:00:59,122 --> 00:01:00,257
seen from above.

24
00:01:00,607 --> 00:01:04,595
At two metres per second, the string
pulls with two newtons, toward the

25
00:01:04,595 --> 00:01:05,495
centre.

26
00:01:05,570 --> 00:01:08,138
Now speed up to four metres per second.

27
00:01:08,138 --> 00:01:09,710
Watch the orange arrow.

28
00:01:10,110 --> 00:01:12,436
The tension jumps to eight newtons.

29
00:01:12,436 --> 00:01:15,067
Double the speed, four times the pull.

30
00:01:15,467 --> 00:01:18,027
On the graph, the dot climbs a curve.

31
00:01:18,027 --> 00:01:20,186
The tension grows with v squared.

32
00:01:20,686 --> 00:01:21,847
Slow-mo replay.

33
00:01:21,847 --> 00:01:23,639
Now we cut the string.

34
00:01:23,989 --> 00:01:25,216
The pull is gone.

35
00:01:25,216 --> 00:01:27,192
The stone does not fly outward.

36
00:01:27,192 --> 00:01:29,819
It carries straight on, along the tangent.

37
00:01:30,219 --> 00:01:32,730
The string alone was bending its path.

38
00:01:32,730 --> 00:01:35,486
That inward pull was
the centripetal force.

39
00:01:36,136 --> 00:01:38,527
Now let's write it down, step by step.

40
00:01:38,877 --> 00:01:39,797
Step one.

41
00:01:39,797 --> 00:01:44,898
At speed v on a circle of radius r,
the acceleration is v squared over

42
00:01:44,898 --> 00:01:46,737
r, toward the centre.

43
00:01:47,487 --> 00:01:48,394
Step two.

44
00:01:48,394 --> 00:01:49,920
Newton's second law.

45
00:01:49,920 --> 00:01:53,148
The net force equals
mass times acceleration.

46
00:01:53,898 --> 00:01:54,947
Step three.

47
00:01:54,947 --> 00:01:59,313
So the net inward force, F
c, is m v squared over r.

48
00:01:59,313 --> 00:02:03,326
With v equal to omega r,
it is m omega squared r.

49
00:02:04,076 --> 00:02:04,976
Step four.

50
00:02:04,975 --> 00:02:05,875
Units.

51
00:02:05,605 --> 00:02:09,625
Kilograms times metres per
second squared, which is newtons.

52
00:02:10,375 --> 00:02:11,500
Remember this.

53
00:02:11,500 --> 00:02:14,148
Centripetal force is not a new force.

54
00:02:14,148 --> 00:02:17,134
It is the net inward pull of real forces.

55
00:02:17,834 --> 00:02:19,367
Now, side by side.

56
00:02:19,717 --> 00:02:22,429
On a string, tension pulls the stone in.

57
00:02:22,429 --> 00:02:25,878
Spin faster and it just
pulls harder, until it snaps.

58
00:02:26,278 --> 00:02:31,467
On a flat road, only friction pushes
the car in, and friction has a limit,

59
00:02:31,467 --> 00:02:32,367
mu m g.

60
00:02:32,284 --> 00:02:35,670
So m v squared over r
can be at most mu m g.

61
00:02:36,270 --> 00:02:37,170
Cancel m.

62
00:02:37,137 --> 00:02:41,083
The top speed is root mu r
g, and the mass has gone.

63
00:02:41,683 --> 00:02:42,731
Now you try.

64
00:02:42,731 --> 00:02:46,517
A heavy truck takes the same
flat curve as a small car.

65
00:02:46,517 --> 00:02:49,509
Is its top safe speed lower, or the same?

66
00:02:49,509 --> 00:02:50,811
Pause and decide.

67
00:02:54,011 --> 00:02:54,911
The same!

68
00:02:54,748 --> 00:02:58,915
The truck needs more friction, but
it presses harder on the road, so it

69
00:02:58,915 --> 00:02:59,815
gets more.

70
00:03:00,347 --> 00:03:01,247
One more.

71
00:03:01,159 --> 00:03:03,248
The Moon goes round the Earth.

72
00:03:03,248 --> 00:03:05,575
What supplies its centripetal force?

73
00:03:05,575 --> 00:03:06,793
Pause and think.

74
00:03:09,993 --> 00:03:10,893
Gravity!

75
00:03:10,755 --> 00:03:13,354
The Earth's pull does the whole job.

76
00:03:13,354 --> 00:03:15,287
No extra force is needed.

77
00:03:15,937 --> 00:03:18,257
So why do Aarav's tyres slide out?

78
00:03:18,257 --> 00:03:19,537
Follow the links.

79
00:03:19,887 --> 00:03:20,787
One.

80
00:03:20,459 --> 00:03:25,508
To go round, the car needs an
inward force of m v squared over r.

81
00:03:25,908 --> 00:03:26,808
Two.

82
00:03:26,414 --> 00:03:30,827
On a flat road, only friction
can give it, and at most mu m g.

83
00:03:31,227 --> 00:03:32,127
Three.

84
00:03:31,834 --> 00:03:36,049
As the car speeds up, the force
it needs grows with v squared.

85
00:03:36,449 --> 00:03:37,349
Four.

86
00:03:36,970 --> 00:03:40,259
Past the limit, friction
cannot bend the path enough.

87
00:03:40,259 --> 00:03:42,741
The car runs wide, toward the tangent.

88
00:03:42,741 --> 00:03:44,218
Nothing pushed it out.

89
00:03:44,818 --> 00:03:46,554
Now, one worked example.

90
00:03:46,904 --> 00:03:50,334
Aarav and his bike have a
mass of sixty kilograms.

91
00:03:50,334 --> 00:03:54,992
He takes a flat ten metre turn at
five metres per second, with mu zero

92
00:03:54,992 --> 00:03:55,934
point four.

93
00:03:55,934 --> 00:03:57,040
Will he skid?

94
00:03:57,540 --> 00:04:00,256
Friction needed, m v squared over r.

95
00:04:00,256 --> 00:04:02,802
Sixty times twenty five, over ten.

96
00:04:02,802 --> 00:04:04,969
One hundred and fifty newtons.

97
00:04:05,719 --> 00:04:07,772
Friction available, mu m g.

98
00:04:07,772 --> 00:04:11,510
Zero point four, times sixty,
times nine point eight.

99
00:04:11,510 --> 00:04:14,087
About two hundred thirty five newtons.

100
00:04:14,837 --> 00:04:17,901
Needed is less than
available, so he turns safely.

101
00:04:18,651 --> 00:04:20,873
His top speed is root mu r g.

102
00:04:20,873 --> 00:04:25,909
The root of thirty nine point two,
about six point three metres per second.

103
00:04:26,559 --> 00:04:28,025
Now, the exam trap.

104
00:04:28,375 --> 00:04:31,635
Many students draw two
forces on the whirling stone.

105
00:04:31,635 --> 00:04:34,337
Tension, and a separate centripetal force.

106
00:04:34,687 --> 00:04:36,631
But there is only one force.

107
00:04:36,631 --> 00:04:39,011
The tension is the centripetal force.

108
00:04:39,661 --> 00:04:41,032
Where is this asked?

109
00:04:41,032 --> 00:04:44,916
In numericals on string tension,
and top speed on a flat curve.

110
00:04:45,516 --> 00:04:46,676
Exam level now.

111
00:04:46,676 --> 00:04:49,923
A disc turns at a steady
five radians per second.

112
00:04:49,923 --> 00:04:53,849
For a coin on it, mu is zero
point five, and g is ten.

113
00:04:53,849 --> 00:04:57,597
How far from the centre can
the coin sit without slipping?

114
00:04:57,597 --> 00:04:58,807
Pause and solve.

115
00:05:04,207 --> 00:05:05,628
The answer is B.

116
00:05:05,628 --> 00:05:07,082
Twenty centimetres.

117
00:05:07,582 --> 00:05:12,741
Friction gives the centripetal force,
so m omega squared r is at most mu

118
00:05:12,741 --> 00:05:13,641
m g.

119
00:05:13,881 --> 00:05:14,909
Cancel m.

120
00:05:14,909 --> 00:05:18,481
So r is at most mu g over omega squared.

121
00:05:18,981 --> 00:05:21,182
That is five, over twenty five.

122
00:05:21,182 --> 00:05:24,124
Zero point two metres,
or twenty centimetres.

123
00:05:24,724 --> 00:05:26,188
Here is your recap.

124
00:05:26,188 --> 00:05:27,692
Screenshot this card.

125
00:05:30,892 --> 00:05:33,729
Can you answer these three
questions without help?

126
00:05:34,129 --> 00:05:35,029
One.

127
00:05:34,639 --> 00:05:38,549
What supplies the centripetal
force for a stone on a string?

128
00:05:39,449 --> 00:05:40,349
Two.

129
00:05:39,977 --> 00:05:44,276
Why does a car run wide on a
flat curve if it goes too fast?

130
00:05:45,175 --> 00:05:46,075
Three.

131
00:05:45,804 --> 00:05:50,173
Why does the top safe speed on
a flat curve not depend on mass?

132
00:05:51,273 --> 00:05:55,855
Next time: how can a tilted road
turn a car with no friction at all?

133
00:05:55,855 --> 00:05:58,092
We find out in banking of roads.
